From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Carry stereo content protocol version 7: the person's element limit

kde_stereo_depth_v1 gains the event element_limit at version 7: how far an
element drawn inside a surface may lie in front of or behind what it lies on,
in pixels of a view 1920 wide, the user's element limit (queue row 121). It
is sent before output_width and depth_limits, which ends the group. Keep every
request and event of the earlier versions. The contract test follows:
version 7, the new event's form, and the events' order, which is their
wire opcodes.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5

diff --git a/src/protocols/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml b/src/protocols/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
index f1d371f..a87ddb0 100644
--- a/src/protocols/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
+++ b/src/protocols/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@
     SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT-CMU
     ]]></copyright>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_content_manager_v1" version="6">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_content_manager_v1" version="7">
   <description summary="declare stereo content of surfaces">
     A program tells the compositor that a surface's picture holds two
     views packed full side by side, with the left view first.
@@ -15,7 +15,8 @@
 
     Since version 3, a program may also follow a surface's desktop depth
     with get_depth, to draw the elements inside the surface at their own
-    depths.
+    depths; since version 7 it is also told the user's element limit, the
+    step of depth inside a window.
 
     Clients may use this protocol to declare stereo content. The version 1
     wire values are stable; future requests must use a new interface version.
@@ -52,7 +53,7 @@
   </request>
 </interface>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_content_v1" version="6">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_content_v1" version="7">
   <description summary="stereo content declaration of one surface">
     The declaration of one surface, created with
     kde_stereo_content_manager_v1.create.
@@ -68,6 +69,7 @@
 
     Version 3 changes nothing in this interface.
     Version 4 adds a double-buffered native source rectangle for each view.
+    Versions 5 to 7 change nothing in this interface.
   </description>
 
   <enum name="error">
@@ -125,7 +127,7 @@
   </request>
 </interface>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="6">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="7">
   <description summary="desktop depth of one surface">
     The compositor shows the desktop's own surfaces at depth levels: the
     active window at the screen, the windows lying behind it sunk, menus,
@@ -149,6 +151,12 @@
     depth on the same screen. Before version 5 the client reads the user's
     settings itself.
 
+    Since version 7 the compositor also tells the client the user's element
+    limit (element_limit): how far an element drawn inside the surface may
+    lie in front of or behind what it lies on, a pixel or so, where the
+    depth limits above are for the desktop's own surfaces. A toolkit that
+    draws a surface's elements at their own depths takes its steps from it.
+
     A surface may have any number of these objects. The depth state is sent
     to each of them. The surface is marked as desktop depth while any of
     its objects marks it, and its place is the one set most recently by any
@@ -272,6 +280,25 @@
     <arg name="width" type="uint" summary="selected output width in pixels per view, or zero"/>
   </event>
 
+  <event name="element_limit" since="7">
+    <description summary="The element limit on the surface's output">
+      Sent to resources of version 7 or newer immediately before
+      output_width, so before every depth_limits event, and whenever the
+      value changes; depth_limits ends the group, and all three describe the
+      same selected output.
+
+      limit is how far an element drawn inside the surface (a button, a
+      field, a page) may lie in front of or behind the surface it lies on,
+      in pixels of a view 1920 wide: the user's element limit, one step of
+      depth inside a window. Zero means the elements inside the surface lie
+      flat. The compositor gives it as the user set it; a client converts
+      it to its buffer's pixels as it converts the depth limits, through the
+      shared stereo_depth_limits_for_buffer conversion, and never rescales
+      it by the size of its window or of an element.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="limit" type="fixed" summary="the element limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+  </event>
+
 </interface>
 
 </protocol>
diff --git a/tests/virtual-stereo-contract.py b/tests/virtual-stereo-contract.py
index 90c0349..fbf6c95 100644
--- a/tests/virtual-stereo-contract.py
+++ b/tests/virtual-stereo-contract.py
@@ -17,14 +17,14 @@ class VirtualStereoContract(unittest.TestCase):
     def test_desktop_depth(self):
         protocol = ET.parse(ROOT / 'kde-stereo-content-v1.xml')
         manager = protocol.find("interface[@name='kde_stereo_content_manager_v1']")
-        self.assertEqual(manager.get('version'), '6')
-        self.assertEqual(protocol.find("interface[@name='kde_stereo_content_v1']").get('version'), '6')
+        self.assertEqual(manager.get('version'), '7')
+        self.assertEqual(protocol.find("interface[@name='kde_stereo_content_v1']").get('version'), '7')
         get_depth = manager.find("request[@name='get_depth']")
         self.assertEqual(get_depth.get('since'), '3')
         self.assertEqual([(a.get('name'), a.get('type'), a.get('interface')) for a in get_depth.findall('arg')],
                          [('id', 'new_id', 'kde_stereo_depth_v1'), ('surface', 'object', 'wl_surface')])
         depth = protocol.find("interface[@name='kde_stereo_depth_v1']")
-        self.assertEqual(depth.get('version'), '6')
+        self.assertEqual(depth.get('version'), '7')
         limits = depth.find("event[@name='depth_limits']")
         self.assertEqual(limits.get('since'), '5')
         self.assertEqual([(a.get('name'), a.get('type')) for a in limits.findall('arg')],
@@ -32,6 +32,12 @@ class VirtualStereoContract(unittest.TestCase):
         width = depth.find("event[@name='output_width']")
         self.assertEqual(width.get('since'), '6')
         self.assertEqual([(a.get('name'), a.get('type')) for a in width.findall('arg')], [('width', 'uint')])
+        element = depth.find("event[@name='element_limit']")
+        self.assertEqual(element.get('since'), '7')
+        self.assertEqual([(a.get('name'), a.get('type')) for a in element.findall('arg')], [('limit', 'fixed')])
+        # an event's opcode is its place: new events only ever join at the end
+        self.assertEqual([e.get('name') for e in depth.findall('event')],
+                         ['depth_state', 'depth_limits', 'output_width', 'element_limit'])
         state = depth.find("event[@name='depth_state']")
         self.assertEqual([(a.get('name'), a.get('type')) for a in state.findall('arg')], [('awake', 'uint'), ('output_edges', 'uint')])
         edges = depth.find("enum[@name='edge']")
